window.__SITE_DATA__={"base":"/","preview":true,"search":[{"id":"AI-01","number":1,"line":"A","title":"Buying AI licences and calling it a strategy","short":"Strategy, not licences","summary":"Define the business change before treating tool access as progress.","text":"A rollout plan is substituting for a business hypothesis when it names licences, training and usage targets but cannot identify the work that should change. The missing decision is not necessarily which technology to buy. It may be who receives the benefit, what constraint matters and who can change the process. Separate exploratory access from a committed business improvement programme. Which business outcome is this initiative meant to change, for whom, and against what baseline? Which workflow, decision or service will work differently—not merely faster within the same step? Who owns the benefit and can change the process, staffing priorities or service design? Write a one-page use-case charter naming the problem, affected users, current baseline, proposed change and accountable business owner. Choose a bounded workflow and state what evidence would justify continuing, changing or stopping; keep a separate budget for open-ended learning. Compare AI with process simplification, existing software and doing nothing before committing to a delivery architecture. Levie’s supplied post distinguishes compute consumption from useful results and says the answer depends on individual workflows. It is a useful challenge to an adoption dashboard: a rising activity line still needs an explanation of which business work improved. The Reshuffle note shifts attention from automating separate tasks to assembling them into a useful service. Reducing one task’s cost may move the bottleneck to integration, trust, demand or accountable delivery. This is a reason to measure the larger outcome as well as the local improvement. The Commoncog note describes effectual reasoning: begin with available means, take an affordable action, learn from it and adjust as partners or constraints become clearer. This supports a pilot designed to answer a decision-relevant question rather than one expected to validate an already fixed plan.","url":"/stations/01-licences-are-not-strategy/"},{"id":"AI-02","number":2,"line":"A","title":"Automating a process nobody understands","short":"Understand the work","summary":"Map real work, including its exceptions and informal decisions, before deciding what to automate.","text":"A workflow diagram can be technically correct and operationally false. Work may actually pass through private spreadsheets, calls, queues and improvised interpretations. Some missing steps are undocumented knowledge; others are unresolved decisions about how the business should operate. Distinguish those two situations so that an engineer is not asked to invent policy while building an integration. Can a person follow one ordinary case and one difficult case from trigger to accepted completion? Where does the work wait, change format, need clarification or rely on an unnamed expert? Which steps are unnecessary, and which apparent inefficiencies protect quality, relationships or accountability? Observe real cases with frontline workers; capture inputs, transformations, handoffs, waits, decisions, exceptions and completion evidence. Assign business owners to unresolved rules before implementation; distinguish historical practice from approved policy. Redesign the smallest useful end-to-end slice, comparing elimination, simplification and automation rather than preserving every existing step. Ajzenstadt’s forecast-variance example requires agreement about forecasts, actuals, thresholds and who receives the result. Some of the apparent implementation work is therefore deciding how the business should operate. An agent cannot reliably recover a policy that the organisation has never agreed. Pierce’s guide starts with an inventory of workflows and asks how work starts, how it moves and what completion means. It then separates tools to retain, absorb or remove. The useful contribution is the sequence of diagnostic questions, rather than the assumption that every business needs one replacement system.","url":"/stations/02-understand-before-automating/"},{"id":"AI-03","number":3,"line":"A","title":"Choosing projects for spectacle rather than business value","short":"Value over spectacle","summary":"Choose useful problems and make room for bounded discovery—not just impressive demonstrations.","text":"A project can be visually impressive yet disconnected from a meaningful constraint. A small task can also be too small to matter. The decision should consider frequency, consequence, feasibility, adoption effort and the ability to turn a local gain into a better service. Separate improvements to current work from experiments that may reveal an entirely new capability. Who will repeatedly use the result, and what work becomes easier or newly possible? Does the proposed change relieve the current constraint or produce more work for another queue? Is this an optimisation project with a known outcome or an exploration whose first deliverable is information? Build a short opportunity portfolio scored against customer value, workflow friction, risk, data access and practical ownership; keep scoring transparent rather than pretend it is precise prediction. Reserve a bounded exploration lane for ideas that do not yet have a stable business case; ask what each experiment taught. Prefer a credible route from a small win to a useful operating change over isolated demos that cannot be maintained or adopted. Pearson’s editorial-workflow example distinguishes lower preparation effort from the ability to reconsider more research and produce more tailored work. Those are different benefits: one releases capacity; the other expands a feasible service. Neither should automatically be translated into cash savings. The Commoncog note describes effectual reasoning: begin with available means, take an affordable action, learn from it and adjust as partners or constraints become clearer. This supports a pilot designed to answer a decision-relevant question rather than one expected to validate an already fixed plan. Pearson’s curiosity essay presents interestingness as an early search signal when the eventual application is not yet known. As a counterweight to rigid portfolio scoring, it supports reserving some bounded exploration space rather than requiring every learning activity to promise immediate financial return.","url":"/stations/03-value-before-spectacle/"},{"id":"AI-04","number":4,"line":"A","title":"Building an agent when simpler software would do","short":"Use simpler software","summary":"Use AI where interpretation helps and ordinary software where explicit rules are sufficient.","text":"An agent-first design introduces choices and failure paths even when the task is already well specified. Conversely, forcing all ambiguous work into rigid rules can create an unmaintainable exception tree. Split the workflow by what each step requires: retrieval, deterministic computation, interpretation, decision authority or action. Select the simplest complete system that meets the acceptance criteria. Which steps genuinely require interpretation rather than lookup, arithmetic or an explicit rule? Can an existing service or a small workflow solve the problem without autonomous planning? Does each additional agent or tool interaction add useful capability worth its cost and failure surface? Prototype a non-agent baseline, such as search plus a form, deterministic extraction checks or rules-based routing. Separate probabilistic interpretation from calculations, validation and authorised writes; make the boundary inspectable. Increase autonomy only when representative tests show a clear benefit over the simpler alternative, including maintenance and review effort. Tan’s supplied talk distinguishes interpretation of ambiguous intent from arithmetic, database operations and constraint handling. His examples place the latter in scripts and structured tools rather than ask language generation to be the calculation engine. Newport’s late-2025 assessment separates text-native coding environments from heterogeneous interfaces and underspecified real-world requests. The useful diagnostic questions concern hidden preferences, observable state and failures across dependent steps—not whether an agent can explain a plausible plan. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools.","url":"/stations/04-simplest-effective-system/"},{"id":"AI-05","number":5,"line":"A","title":"Measuring AI activity instead of business improvement","short":"Measure outcomes","summary":"Connect usage to accepted work and accepted work to an actual business outcome.","text":"Logins, tokens, drafts and generated code measure activity. They can help diagnose adoption and cost but do not establish quality, use or value. Even time saved is incomplete until the organisation understands what happens to the released capacity. Define a chain from participation to changed workflow to accepted output to the result the business or customer values. What was the pre-change baseline, and are the compared cases similar in difficulty and volume? Does “completed” mean generated, reviewed, accepted, used or resolved for the customer? Where are checking, correction, escalation and displaced downstream work included? Choose an existing business measure, add quality and risk guardrails, and define the unit of accepted work before rollout. Collect active effort and end-to-end time separately; use a comparison design appropriate to the decision and document its limitations. Record whether released capacity becomes more throughput, better service, less overtime or a realised cost change; do not count all of these as the same benefit. Levie’s supplied post distinguishes compute consumption from useful results and says the answer depends on individual workflows. It is a useful challenge to an adoption dashboard: a rising activity line still needs an explanation of which business work improved. The supplied Work at the Frontier note describes people using AI for tasks beyond the conventional boundaries of their occupations. Its evidence concerns patterns in messages and occupational classification, not whether the resulting work was correct, adopted or economically valuable. The supplied Cybernetic Teammate research note reports a randomised product-innovation exercise in which AI-assisted individuals could perform comparably to unassisted teams on assessed ideas. This is a useful example of testing work quality rather than counting usage, while preserving the boundary between an exercise and sustained organisational performance.","url":"/stations/05-measure-business-improvement/"},{"id":"AI-06","number":6,"line":"A","title":"Optimising model cost before proving the task works—or ignoring economics once it does","short":"Prove the economics","summary":"Establish capability inside a budget, then optimise the complete cost of successful delivery.","text":"A cheap model that fails does not prove a use case is infeasible, and an expensive configuration that works does not prove it is economical. Separate the feasibility question from the operating-cost question without postponing all financial discipline. Test a sufficiently capable baseline, identify why it succeeds or fails, and then compare cheaper or simpler configurations against the same quality boundary. Is the initial configuration capable enough to test the idea, or is cost minimisation masking feasibility? Which costs sit outside model tokens: retrieval, integrations, retries, human review and support? Can spending grow through loops, concurrent jobs or unexpected demand even if each request looks cheap? Allocate a bounded feasibility budget and define acceptable completion before comparing model configurations. Benchmark complete alternatives on representative work; include routing, deterministic components and escalation rather than only model substitution. Install per-task and aggregate spending limits, then revisit economics when volume, quality, prices or review requirements change. The source note links cost per successful task with internal evaluations and a system built from context, tools and instructions. This supports evaluating complete task configurations and making quality criteria explicit before interpreting a lower generation bill as progress. Acharya’s discussion calls for practical familiarity with different models and connects selection to the task’s upside and verifiability. A useful application is to compare configurations on the same accepted-work definition rather than presume the cheapest or most capable model is always the economical choice. Pearson’s editorial-workflow example distinguishes lower preparation effort from the ability to reconsider more research and produce more tailored work. Those are different benefits: one releases capacity; the other expands a feasible service. Neither should automatically be translated into cash savings.","url":"/stations/06-economics-after-feasibility/"},{"id":"AI-07","number":7,"line":"B","title":"Treating AI transformation as an IT project or one person’s responsibility","short":"Business ownership","summary":"Join executive sponsorship, process ownership and technical delivery without pretending one role can do all three.","text":"A technical team can build a service without having authority to resolve business priorities, change incentives or assign responsibility across departments. An executive sponsor can create urgency without owning day-to-day decisions. State which decisions sit with the sponsor, the process owner and the operational owner; give each the resources needed to carry that responsibility. Who can make cross-functional tradeoffs about the outcome, risk and workflow design? Does the named owner have authority and time, or only accountability after a failure? Which responsibilities would be uncovered if the AI specialist or sponsor left tomorrow? Name a business outcome owner, an executive escalation route and a delivery/operating owner for each initiative. Write decision rights for prioritisation, data access, acceptance, release, budget and exceptions; include affected functions rather than a ceremonial steering group. Review whether management work has genuinely disappeared or has moved into informal coordination, coaching and conflict resolution. Block’s proposed architecture includes individuals accountable for particular customer outcomes who can draw resources from several capabilities, alongside specialists and player-coaches. The useful design question is how outcome responsibility and resource authority are joined after information-routing work changes. The book’s decentralisation guidance pairs local decision-making with executive guardrails, clear decision lanes, a changed coaching role for managers and removal of cross-team blockers. Distributed work therefore does not mean that each team invents its own risk policy or loses access to shared expertise. Foss argues that firms and markets must be compared as ways to govern specific transactions. Adaptation when conditions change, relationship-specific investments and difficult-to-measure performance remain relevant even when exchanging information becomes easier. This challenges any automatic inference from AI capability to a flat organisation or universal outsourcing. The same post describes questions about cross-functional work, central management of agents, deployment, testing and ownership. Buying access leaves those decisions unresolved. It also identifies fragmented information and uneven internal skills as obstacles that a model choice alone does not address.","url":"/stations/07-business-ownership/"},{"id":"AI-08","number":8,"line":"B","title":"Building a central AI team that becomes everyone’s bottleneck","short":"Avoid the bottleneck","summary":"Centralise reusable support and essential controls while keeping local outcomes and workflow knowledge close to the work.","text":"Central expertise is valuable, but a central team asked to discover, build, approve and support every use case becomes a queue. Unrestricted decentralisation creates a different problem: duplicated integrations, inconsistent controls and unowned applications. Distinguish shared capabilities from local delivery decisions, and make the route for obtaining help explicit. Which requests genuinely need specialist central work, and which are waiting for permission or reusable components? Can business teams build within a supported path without bypassing controls? Who maintains shared context, evaluation tools and integrations after the first team benefits? Publish a central service catalog with supported platforms, reusable patterns, review services, escalation routes and response expectations. Delegate bounded decisions to trained local owners; keep non-negotiable identity, data and release controls common. Fund maintenance of shared capabilities and recurring knowledge exchange, not only the visible backlog of new prototypes. The same post describes questions about cross-functional work, central management of agents, deployment, testing and ownership. Buying access leaves those decisions unresolved. It also identifies fragmented information and uneven internal skills as obstacles that a model choice alone does not address. The book’s decentralisation guidance pairs local decision-making with executive guardrails, clear decision lanes, a changed coaching role for managers and removal of cross-team blockers. Distributed work therefore does not mean that each team invents its own risk policy or loses access to shared expertise. The structured note describes distributed intelligence as depending on shared cross-functional context and a culture reinforced through rewards, roles and actual decisions. Granting nominal autonomy without those supports may simply relocate coordination work to overloaded local teams. Shipper describes moving from personal agents toward agents responsible for team or company jobs, with continuing engineering and maintenance. This offers a concrete explanation for why an individual’s useful experiment still needs a service owner, shared context and ongoing support before others rely on it.","url":"/stations/08-central-team-without-bottleneck/"},{"id":"AI-09","number":9,"line":"B","title":"Expecting licences and a training session to change how people work","short":"Learn in real work","summary":"Develop role-specific capability through real tasks, feedback and protected practice.","text":"A generic course can introduce a tool without helping someone recognise a useful task, check the output or recover from a failure. Users, subject-matter experts, builders, reviewers and operators need different competencies. Training should therefore be connected to the workflow people will actually perform and the responsibility they will carry, not measured only by attendance. What must each role be able to do safely and competently after training? Do employees have approved tools, useful data, examples and time to practise on relevant work? Where can they get help when an output is plausible but wrong or a task crosses their expertise? Define practical skill expectations by role, including verification, escalation and data handling—not just prompting. Use supervised work examples, peer clinics and reusable case libraries; protect learning time in workloads. Check whether people can perform and critique representative tasks, then update the support material from recurring misunderstandings. The supplied Cybernetic Teammate research note reports a randomised product-innovation exercise in which AI-assisted individuals could perform comparably to unassisted teams on assessed ideas. This is a useful example of testing work quality rather than counting usage, while preserving the boundary between an exercise and sustained organisational performance. The supplied Work at the Frontier note describes people using AI for tasks beyond the conventional boundaries of their occupations. Its evidence concerns patterns in messages and occupational classification, not whether the resulting work was correct, adopted or economically valuable. The video-derived note distinguishes reusable skills and subject-matter intent from engineering responsibility, and calls for a governed development lifecycle for non-technical builders. This supports a division between contributing business knowledge, experimenting safely and accepting responsibility for a production service. The interview distinguishes asking worthwhile questions, selecting useful outputs and committing to action. Its execution-trap discussion warns that people can become busier producing artifacts after the bottleneck has moved elsewhere. These distinctions help specify what employees must learn and what accountable reviewers must actually do.","url":"/stations/09-learning-in-real-work/"},{"id":"AI-10","number":10,"line":"B","title":"Letting prototypes become production systems without a governed path","short":"Govern the prototype","summary":"Give non-technical builders a supported route from useful experiment to owned, tested service.","text":"The danger is not that a subject-matter expert creates a useful prototype. It is that a prototype gains users, permissions and business importance without a deliberate promotion decision. A production service needs someone who can explain its dependencies, evaluate changes, handle incidents and support users—even when AI generated much of the implementation. Where is the boundary between a private experiment, a shared pilot and a production dependency? Who can approve promotion, and what evidence must they inspect? Can the system be rebuilt, supported and stopped without the original creator’s personal account? Offer a sandbox and an approved promotion path with increasing controls as users, sensitivity and action authority grow. Require representative tests, named owners, service identities, versioned artifacts, support arrangements and recovery evidence before production use. Let business experts own intent and acceptance examples while involving engineering and risk specialists where the responsibility requires their skills. The video-derived note distinguishes reusable skills and subject-matter intent from engineering responsibility, and calls for a governed development lifecycle for non-technical builders. This supports a division between contributing business knowledge, experimenting safely and accepting responsibility for a production service. The addendum expands citizen development into a release decision supported by quality checks, difficult cases, security review and recovery provisions. It also separates the person who created a prototype from the person accountable for accepting it into production. The supplied 10X cheatsheet describes conventions, project specifications, active work and logs as maintained Markdown artifacts. It also distinguishes a manually asserted completion status from one derived from outstanding work. This is useful for detecting stale context and falsely complete projects.","url":"/stations/10-prototype-to-production/"},{"id":"AI-11","number":11,"line":"B","title":"Bringing Legal, Security, IT and Finance in only to approve or block the finished product","short":"Bring risk partners in","summary":"Design a workable route for responsible experimentation with the people who own its constraints.","text":"Late review turns foreseeable requirements into apparent scope creep and encourages a false choice between speed and control. Early participation is useful when it identifies the real boundary: which data, actions, costs and commitments the experiment may involve. It is not useful when every exploratory question becomes an open-ended approval queue. Which functions will need to accept the operating result, and what information do they need now? Is there an approved low-risk experimentation path with clear data and spending boundaries? Which unresolved requirements could force a redesign after users have already adopted the prototype? Hold an early use-case review with a concrete workflow and action/data map rather than a generic AI presentation. Publish supported tools, allowed data categories, spending boundaries, escalation routes and review criteria for common experiment types. Invite risk and support owners to help design acceptance and incident tests; resolve disagreements through a named business decision-maker. The source note places Legal, HR and IT involvement alongside rollout rather than as a final rejection step. The useful lesson is organisational: agree how useful experimentation can proceed and what information these teams need before a prototype becomes an operating dependency. The compiler’s expanded architecture adds identity, access policy, approval routing, evaluation, cost controls and operational oversight to a basic reasoning loop. These mechanisms belong in the surrounding service and tool interfaces; a generated instruction does not itself enforce them. Foss argues that firms and markets must be compared as ways to govern specific transactions. Adaptation when conditions change, relationship-specific investments and difficult-to-measure performance remain relevant even when exchanging information becomes easier. This challenges any automatic inference from AI capability to a flat organisation or universal outsourcing.","url":"/stations/11-risk-partners-from-start/"},{"id":"AI-12","number":12,"line":"B","title":"Treating resistance as a training problem","short":"Rebuild trust","summary":"Address trust, incentives, workload and role uncertainty rather than assume another course will solve adoption.","text":"People may avoid a system because it previously failed, increases checking work, threatens their status or conflicts with how performance is rewarded. They may also have legitimate concerns about confidentiality or customer impact. Find the specific obstacle before designing the intervention. Honest uncertainty about role changes is more useful than an unsupported promise that nothing will change. What happened during previous pilots, and can employees see how the current design differs? Who gains from the change and who absorbs extra work, scrutiny or uncertainty? Do incentives reward better outcomes and shared learning, or merely visible tool use and individual output? Interview users and non-users about real tasks, prior failures and perceived costs; separate training, access, design and trust problems. Explain what is known and unknown about roles, decision authority and workforce implications; involve employees in redesigning the work. Protect practice time, recognise useful corrections and make it safe to report defects or workload increases without being labelled anti-AI. Evans argues that jobs and the markets around them can change when a task becomes cheaper: demand, adjacent activities and business models also respond. This is a reason to discuss work redesign honestly without promising either that no roles will change or that a task-level capability implies a specific headcount reduction. The structured note describes distributed intelligence as depending on shared cross-functional context and a culture reinforced through rewards, roles and actual decisions. Granting nominal autonomy without those supports may simply relocate coordination work to overloaded local teams. Brooks’s supplied opinion piece raises the possibility that people use saved time to take on more work and supervise more parallel activity. It is a prompt to investigate workload, concentration and learning during rollout rather than assume faster generation automatically improves employee experience.","url":"/stations/12-resistance-incentives-trust/"},{"id":"AI-13","number":13,"line":"B","title":"Outsourcing judgement along with the task","short":"Keep your judgement","summary":"Keep the capacity to judge the work, challenge its assumptions and accept responsibility for its consequences.","text":"A system can produce an apparently complete answer without deciding whether the question was useful, the evidence sufficient or the action appropriate. Human review also fails when the reviewer lacks expertise, time, independence or authority. Specify the judgement that remains necessary and preserve opportunities for people to learn it; a nominal approval button does not do that work. What must a competent reviewer understand beyond the final generated output? Can the reviewer reject, change or escalate the result, and do they have time to inspect the evidence? Which learning opportunities disappear when routine work is automated, and how will future experts develop? Define reviewer responsibilities around evidence, context, assumptions, quality and consequences; do not reduce review to stylistic editing. Provide the relevant source material and action preview, and route cases according to consequence and uncertainty rather than convenience. Build supervised practice, challenge exercises and feedback into roles so that automation does not consume the expertise needed to oversee it. The interview distinguishes asking worthwhile questions, selecting useful outputs and committing to action. Its execution-trap discussion warns that people can become busier producing artifacts after the bottleneck has moved elsewhere. These distinctions help specify what employees must learn and what accountable reviewers must actually do. The discussion explains why automating many tasks in a role does not settle the outcome: one remaining task may constrain reliability or be the service the customer values. This helps separate average task performance from the failure that makes a completed workflow unacceptable. The compiler’s critical discussion warns that agents can produce more proposed changes than experts can inspect and that codification can remove learning opportunities for future experts. A sustainable operating model must fund both genuine review and the development of people capable of performing it. The companion material describes an operating manual and a visible review surface for proposed email or customer actions. A person owns exceptional cases and approvals; the agent’s intermediate work remains inspectable. This offers a concrete alternative to an opaque personal automation.","url":"/stations/13-retain-judgement/"},{"id":"AI-14","number":14,"line":"C","title":"Waiting for perfect data—or assuming AI will repair the data for you","short":"Fix useful data","summary":"Prepare the data needed for the chosen use case without making every project depend on a complete enterprise rebuild.","text":"Two opposite assumptions create delay or failure: that all data must be cleaned before any experiment, or that a model can compensate for missing, stale and inaccessible information. Determine the minimum trustworthy input for the chosen scope. A read-only experiment may be possible before a major migration, but its source limits and the cost of adapting it later must remain visible. Which specific data defects prevent this use case from meeting its acceptance criteria? Can the useful scope be narrowed while preserving permissions, freshness and a clear explanation of limitations? What changes when the source system migrates, and who will maintain the adapter or replacement? Create a use-case data contract naming required fields, definitions, coverage, freshness, access and known gaps. Repair or constrain the blocking issues first; test representative extracts before authorising consequential actions. Consider staged read-only access, a replica or an approved integration where appropriate, documenting lag, reconciliation and migration dependencies rather than assuming them away. Levie connects fragmented enterprise data with the need for company-specific context and discusses interest in multiple models. The design question is which context, tools and definitions should remain under company control when the model changes. The guide proposes moving one department at a time and stabilising its daily work before proceeding. That is an example of a bounded migration unit and adoption checkpoint. It suggests asking what must remain interoperable while old and new workflows coexist. The Reshuffle note shifts attention from automating separate tasks to assembling them into a useful service. Reducing one task’s cost may move the bottleneck to integration, trust, demand or accountable delivery. This is a reason to measure the larger outcome as well as the local improvement. The supplied analysis warns that a cited artifact may still be outdated, inapplicable or incomplete. Persistent memory also needs correction and expiry. This is especially useful for answers about exceptions: finding a previous decision should trigger an applicability check rather than automatic reuse.","url":"/stations/14-use-case-data-foundation/"},{"id":"AI-15","number":15,"line":"C","title":"Expecting consistent answers from inconsistent business definitions","short":"Define the terms","summary":"Make business meaning, calculation rules and source authority explicit for each question.","text":"When departments use the same word differently, a model can produce a coherent answer under the wrong interpretation. The defect is not necessarily bad retrieval or arithmetic. Define the relevant entity, measure, time basis and scope, including situations where more than one definition is legitimately needed. A single “source of truth” slogan does not resolve a genuine business disagreement. Which measures have multiple valid meanings, such as orders, billings, revenue or active customers? Which reporting period, currency, status and organisational scope apply to the question? Who can approve a definition and decide how conflicts or restatements are handled? Build a small business glossary tied to real questions, calculations and source fields rather than a disconnected terminology project. Version consequential definitions and record authority, effective date and applicability; preserve legitimate alternative measures with clear names. Make the system request clarification when a material ambiguity remains, and include definition changes in regression tests. The supplied note connects reusable expertise with shared entities, synonyms, regional context and policy meaning. A semantic layer is therefore more than a dictionary: it should explain which interpretation applies in a particular business setting. The context-graphs note distinguishes operational objects and their current state from the evidence, policy, exceptions and approvals behind a decision. That distinction exposes missing context in many integrations: two identical-looking outcomes may have been produced under different rules. Tan’s talk explicitly asks what should be recorded, how it should be linked, what stays in active memory and who resolves contradictions. These questions are useful even without adopting his particular personal system: retrieval cannot choose the organisation’s authority hierarchy for it. Ajzenstadt’s forecast-variance example requires agreement about forecasts, actuals, thresholds and who receives the result. Some of the apparent implementation work is therefore deciding how the business should operate. An agent cannot reliably recover a policy that the organisation has never agreed.","url":"/stations/15-shared-business-definitions/"},{"id":"AI-16","number":16,"line":"C","title":"Treating an available data feed as the whole picture","short":"Know the blind spots","summary":"Make the boundary of the evidence visible: coverage, missingness, history and freshness.","text":"An answer can be correct for a source but misleading for the business. The source may exclude certain customers, historical periods, events or fields. Absence in an extract does not establish absence in reality. Treat the feed’s scope as part of the answer and define when insufficient coverage requires abstention, clarification or another data source. Which customers, regions, products and periods are not represented? Are missing values genuine negatives, unavailable fields, failed ingestion or a permissions effect? Can the user tell what the answer includes, excludes and assumes about freshness? Publish a coverage manifest for each source and connect it to the retrieval or answering workflow. Test known-absent, partially covered and stale cases; require the system to distinguish “not found here” from “does not exist.” Monitor ingestion and schema changes so that coverage loss becomes an operational event rather than an invisible change in answers. Sphere’s supplied note distinguishes expertise itself from the artifacts left when experts act. It describes retrieving evidence, returning cited answers and having experts validate them. That is useful when institutional knowledge is dispersed across past work, while preserving the difference between a trace and complete understanding. The supplied analysis warns that a cited artifact may still be outdated, inapplicable or incomplete. Persistent memory also needs correction and expiry. This is especially useful for answers about exceptions: finding a previous decision should trigger an applicability check rather than automatic reuse. The compiler’s risk analysis identifies policy drift, incomplete capture, biased precedent and poorly governed reuse as dangers in decision histories. A prior exception should remain tied to its scope and authority; it should not silently become the default rule for later cases. The supplied Work at the Frontier note describes people using AI for tasks beyond the conventional boundaries of their occupations. Its evidence concerns patterns in messages and occupational classification, not whether the resulting work was correct, adopted or economically valuable.","url":"/stations/16-data-coverage-and-limits/"},{"id":"AI-17","number":17,"line":"C","title":"Leaving essential business rules in people’s heads","short":"Capture the know-how","summary":"Capture decision-relevant expertise while preserving the people and practice that keep it current.","text":"A process can depend on an experienced colleague’s recognition of exceptions that never appear in a manual. Capturing this knowledge requires cases, reasoning and applicability—not just recording a meeting. Historical practice must also be distinguished from current policy. Assign stewardship and preserve opportunities for future experts to learn through supervised work. Which decisions repeatedly require a particular person, and what makes those cases unusual? Can experts explain the evidence, tradeoffs and circumstances under which a rule should not apply? Who validates, updates or retires captured knowledge after the original expert leaves? Prioritise consequential, recurring decisions and interview experts using real contrasting cases rather than abstract requests to document everything. Store the reasoning, sources, authority, exceptions, scope and known limits with each reusable rule; obtain expert review. Attach ownership, review triggers and regression cases, while maintaining apprenticeship and a route for new evidence to challenge established practice. The CMR source note describes identifying critical expertise and making context-dependent reasoning visible through cases and exceptions. The practical opportunity is to prioritise knowledge that repeatedly changes important decisions, rather than launch an indiscriminate document-everything programme. The CMR note gives people an ongoing role in checking cases, challenging outputs and maintaining business relevance. Capturing an expert’s reasoning is the start of a stewardship process, not evidence that the expert’s role or the need for future learning has disappeared. Sphere’s supplied note distinguishes expertise itself from the artifacts left when experts act. It describes retrieving evidence, returning cited answers and having experts validate them. That is useful when institutional knowledge is dispersed across past work, while preserving the difference between a trace and complete understanding. The notes describe comparing accumulated guidance with advice given in real interactions. That is useful for identifying outdated procedure and exceptions, but the observed advice still needs review before becoming a general rule. Recording more interactions also raises consent and access questions.","url":"/stations/17-capture-and-renew-expertise/"},{"id":"AI-18","number":18,"line":"C","title":"Making the model read everything to find one thing","short":"Retrieve what matters","summary":"Provide permission-aware retrieval that returns the right evidence and makes retrieval failures diagnosable.","text":"A large context window does not decide what evidence matters, whether the correct source was found or which user may see it. Give the model tools suited to the task: search, filtering, structured queries and progressive detail. Evaluate retrieval independently enough to tell whether an incorrect answer came from missing evidence or misuse of evidence that was available. Can the system retrieve the authoritative record without receiving irrelevant or unauthorised material? Do retrieved results carry source identity, version, date and enough surrounding context to interpret them? Are known retrieval failures represented in tests, including conflicting, missing and access-restricted evidence? Design task-specific search and filtering tools with explicit input/output contracts and enforced permissions. Return relevant passages or records with provenance and enough context to preserve qualifications; allow deeper retrieval when needed. Test retrieval coverage and answer grounding separately, then trace both through end-to-end acceptance cases. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. The supplied analysis warns that a cited artifact may still be outdated, inapplicable or incomplete. Persistent memory also needs correction and expiry. This is especially useful for answers about exceptions: finding a previous decision should trigger an applicability check rather than automatic reuse. Tan’s talk explicitly asks what should be recorded, how it should be linked, what stays in active memory and who resolves contradictions. These questions are useful even without adopting his particular personal system: retrieval cannot choose the organisation’s authority hierarchy for it.","url":"/stations/18-relevant-retrieval/"},{"id":"AI-19","number":19,"line":"C","title":"Assuming connected systems will work together correctly","short":"Test integrations","summary":"Test business semantics and partial effects, not just successful API connections.","text":"A tool can connect successfully while acting on the wrong identity, unit, period or record. Multi-step work adds the possibility that one action succeeds and the next fails. Define how state moves between systems, how retries are recognised and how discrepancies are reconciled. The contract must describe business meaning and failure behaviour as well as technical fields. How are identities, versions, units, currencies and status meanings matched between systems? What happens if the response times out after an action has already succeeded? Can partial completion, duplicate events and out-of-order updates be detected and repaired? Document each tool’s allowed effects, preconditions, success evidence and failure states; keep calculations and validations explicit. Use suitable duplicate-prevention and reconciliation mechanisms for consequential writes, then test timeouts and retries in a safe environment. Validate complete real workflows across source and destination systems, including permission boundaries and downstream acceptance. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. The supplied failure dossier includes unsafe retries, partial transactions, duplicate actions, poisoned context, weak identity boundaries and missing recovery. These are useful test categories because a superficially successful response can coexist with a damaged record or an action executed twice. Newport’s late-2025 assessment separates text-native coding environments from heterogeneous interfaces and underspecified real-world requests. The useful diagnostic questions concern hidden preferences, observable state and failures across dependent steps—not whether an agent can explain a plausible plan.","url":"/stations/19-integration-behaviour/"},{"id":"AI-20","number":20,"line":"D","title":"Confusing instructions with access controls","short":"Enforce permissions","summary":"Enforce what an agent may read and do in software, using identities and permissions appropriate to its job.","text":"A prompt expresses an intended behaviour; it does not remove a capability from the surrounding system. Scope the agent’s actual access, distinguish the requesting user from the service identity and make consequential actions inspectable and attributable. Separate read, write, send, delete and spending authority rather than treating a connector as a single undifferentiated permission. What can the agent actually do with its current credentials, regardless of its instructions? Whose authority is represented by each action, and can it exceed the requesting user’s permitted scope? Can access be revoked or narrowed without relying on the agent to cooperate? Create a capability matrix by task and identity; grant only the data and actions needed for the approved job. Enforce permissions at the service and tool boundary, with appropriate short-lived credentials and approval for consequential actions. Test denied actions and cross-user cases; keep an attributable record of the requester, agent identity, approved scope and executed effect. The compiler’s expanded architecture adds identity, access policy, approval routing, evaluation, cost controls and operational oversight to a basic reasoning loop. These mechanisms belong in the surrounding service and tool interfaces; a generated instruction does not itself enforce them. The video-derived note sets the requesting user’s permissions as an upper bound. The compiler’s addendum separates that human authority from the service identity used by the agent. Together these suggest an implementation with attributable execution and enforcement that the agent does not silently exceed the requester’s allowed scope.","url":"/stations/20-enforced-agent-permissions/"},{"id":"AI-21","number":21,"line":"D","title":"Making data protection depend on employees remembering the rules","short":"Protect data by design","summary":"Make approved data handling understandable and supported by technical controls across the information lifecycle.","text":"Data protection can fail at input, retrieval, output, logs, persistent memory or sharing—not only when someone forgets to remove a name. Identify the permitted purpose and audience at each stage. Account settings and contracts matter, but they need to be connected to an approved working path that people can actually use. Which services and accounts receive company information, and who has checked their permitted use and retention arrangements? Can sensitive information move from a legitimate read into an inappropriate output, trace or shared memory? Are collection, access, retention, correction and deletion responsibilities assigned? Classify the data and approved purposes for each workflow; provide supported accounts and connectors rather than rely on informal personal use. Apply suitable access restrictions and automated input/output checks, and minimise what is retained in traces and memory. Define retention and deletion processes with responsible specialists, then test the real data path—including logs, cached copies and downstream destinations. The addendum discusses information lifecycle and authority to edit, approve or retire shared context. A company can have accurate retrieval and still produce bad answers when an unreviewed definition or obsolete instruction is promoted into its common knowledge layer. The compiler’s risk analysis identifies policy drift, incomplete capture, biased precedent and poorly governed reuse as dangers in decision histories. A prior exception should remain tied to its scope and authority; it should not silently become the default rule for later cases. Tan’s talk explicitly asks what should be recorded, how it should be linked, what stays in active memory and who resolves contradictions. These questions are useful even without adopting his particular personal system: retrieval cannot choose the organisation’s authority hierarchy for it.","url":"/stations/21-data-protection-by-design/"},{"id":"AI-22","number":22,"line":"D","title":"Trusting every instruction an agent encounters","short":"Distrust outside instructions","summary":"Treat retrieved documents, messages and tool results as evidence to inspect—not as authority to change the agent’s rules.","text":"An agent may encounter text that asks it to ignore instructions, disclose information or call a tool. Such text can be part of the task data rather than an authorised command. Design for that distinction and enforce the action boundary outside the model. Prompt wording can help communicate intent but should not be the only defence. Can material from a customer, website, attachment or retrieved record influence tool use beyond its authorised role? Which controls still hold if the model follows a malicious or misleading instruction in that material? Can a contaminated memory or shared instruction file affect future users or workflows? Identify untrusted input surfaces and keep them separate from approved policy and task instructions in the system design. Use constrained tools, enforced permissions and explicit approval for high-consequence effects; do not allow encountered text to grant new access. Test realistic redirection and data-exfiltration attempts in an authorised test environment, including stored-context contamination and cross-document instructions. The compiler’s security analysis includes prompt injection and compromised memory or context. The practical distinction is between content the agent must examine and instructions entitled to govern its actions. Tools should not accept a permission change merely because a document asks the agent to make one. The compiler’s expanded architecture adds identity, access policy, approval routing, evaluation, cost controls and operational oversight to a basic reasoning loop. These mechanisms belong in the surrounding service and tool interfaces; a generated instruction does not itself enforce them. The compiler’s risk analysis identifies policy drift, incomplete capture, biased precedent and poorly governed reuse as dangers in decision histories. A prior exception should remain tied to its scope and authority; it should not silently become the default rule for later cases.","url":"/stations/22-untrusted-instructions/"},{"id":"AI-23","number":23,"line":"D","title":"Letting agents run or spend without hard limits","short":"Set hard limits","summary":"Bound the amount of work, money and external effect an agent can authorise before the system stops or seeks approval.","text":"A per-request price can hide an unbounded loop, repeated retries or many concurrent jobs. Spending exposure also includes payments, subscriptions and external commitments, not only model usage. Define aggregate limits and the behaviour at the boundary before deployment. The stopping mechanism must be enforceable by the host service, not dependent on the agent remembering its budget. What is the maximum aggregate exposure across jobs, retries, teams and connected paid services? Who can change a limit, and is that change recorded and separately authorised? What does the system return or do when time, requests, money or action limits are reached? Set hard limits for the relevant dimensions: runtime, requests, retries, concurrency, paid tools and transactions; include aggregate budgets. Choose a safe boundary behaviour such as pause, partial result or approval request, and ensure it does not create uncontrolled follow-on work. Test exhaustion and runaway scenarios, alert accountable owners and retain enough evidence to understand where resources went. The compiler’s expanded architecture adds identity, access policy, approval routing, evaluation, cost controls and operational oversight to a basic reasoning loop. These mechanisms belong in the surrounding service and tool interfaces; a generated instruction does not itself enforce them. Rao’s conceptual distinction concerns whether acting generates usable information or instead outruns feedback and creates external costs. Applied to an agent, the question is how much it can change before the organisation can observe, interpret and contain an error.","url":"/stations/23-bounded-agent-execution/"},{"id":"AI-24","number":24,"line":"D","title":"Applying the same controls to every use case","short":"Match controls to risk","summary":"Calibrate autonomy and assurance to each decision’s consequences, uncertainty and reversibility.","text":"A drafting tool and a system that changes a customer’s entitlement do not expose people to the same consequences. Even within one workflow, a read-only step and an irreversible action may need different controls. Avoid collapsing task autonomy, user collaboration and organisational readiness into a single maturity score. Assess who can be affected and what recourse exists. Who could be harmed by an incorrect or unfair result, and can the effect be reversed in practice? Which decisions require stronger evidence, specialist review or an opportunity to challenge the outcome? Can the organisation actually monitor and support the level of autonomy being proposed? Classify the workflow’s decisions by consequence, sensitivity, uncertainty, affected population and reversibility, using specialists where needed. Choose task-specific control patterns: assistance, reviewed proposal, constrained execution or stronger approval; make prohibited uses explicit. Test relevant quality, fairness, misuse and recovery concerns, and provide a route for affected users to obtain correction or human review. The discussion explains why automating many tasks in a role does not settle the outcome: one remaining task may constrain reliability or be the service the customer values. This helps separate average task performance from the failure that makes a completed workflow unacceptable.","url":"/stations/24-controls-proportionate-to-risk/"},{"id":"AI-25","number":25,"line":"E","title":"Building before agreeing what a good result looks like","short":"Define good results","summary":"Turn business expectations into representative cases, acceptance criteria and boundaries for abstention.","text":"Without an agreed standard, a team can improve fluent output while disagreeing about whether it is useful or safe. Start with real work and the people who can judge it. Define unacceptable failures and when the correct response is to ask for help. Preserve verified corrections as tests, while avoiding a test set that becomes only a record of past successes. Can domain experts agree on why a result is acceptable, unacceptable or requires clarification? Do the examples reflect the actual range of tasks and important exceptions? What happens to a verified correction after the immediate incident is resolved? Collect representative cases with source evidence, expected behaviour and a rationale; involve business experts in resolving disagreement. Define acceptance at the workflow level, including actions, abstention, authority and downstream use—not only answer style. Version the evaluation set and turn verified failures into regression cases; keep separate cases for development and for independent checks where practical. Every’s companion workflow notes recommend writing evaluation cases before relying on long-running agents, including situations that should not trigger the skill. The retained artifact is not just a better prompt but trigger rules, realistic cases and reusable lessons from verified failures. The twelve-factor note includes evaluation rubrics, retained operational traces and encoded expertise as parts of a learning system. Together they suggest that trace collection is useful only when the organisation can interpret outcomes and revise a tested process. The CMR note gives people an ongoing role in checking cases, challenging outputs and maintaining business relevance. Capturing an expert’s reasoning is the start of a stewardship process, not evidence that the expert’s role or the need for future learning has disappeared.","url":"/stations/25-define-good-results/"},{"id":"AI-26","number":26,"line":"E","title":"Letting the builders test only the happy paths","short":"Test the hard cases","summary":"Use domain users, exceptions and adversarial cases to test the boundaries of the entire workflow.","text":"Builders know the intended path and may unconsciously supply missing context or avoid awkward requests. Real work includes ambiguity, unusual units, unavailable tools, stale rules and requests that should not be executed. Bring people who understand the business consequences into the test process, and test correct refusal and escalation as first-class outcomes. Which cases would an experienced employee hesitate over or send to someone else? Have missing evidence, conflicting rules, denied access, partial completion and unusual units been tested? Can testers challenge assumptions independently of the people who designed the system? Recruit representative domain users and independent reviewers; ask them to supply difficult real cases and explain why they matter. Create a risk-based coverage matrix spanning normal, ambiguous, exceptional, denied and adversarial situations; avoid relying only on random easy examples. Test both the output and actual tool effects, including stop/ask-for-help behaviour; preserve failures with verified expected results. Every’s companion workflow notes recommend writing evaluation cases before relying on long-running agents, including situations that should not trigger the skill. The retained artifact is not just a better prompt but trigger rules, realistic cases and reusable lessons from verified failures. The supplied failure dossier includes unsafe retries, partial transactions, duplicate actions, poisoned context, weak identity boundaries and missing recovery. These are useful test categories because a superficially successful response can coexist with a damaged record or an action executed twice. The compiler’s security analysis includes prompt injection and compromised memory or context. The practical distinction is between content the agent must examine and instructions entitled to govern its actions. Tools should not accept a permission change merely because a document asks the agent to make one. The discussion explains why automating many tasks in a role does not settle the outcome: one remaining task may constrain reliability or be the service the customer values. This helps separate average task performance from the failure that makes a completed workflow unacceptable.","url":"/stations/26-test-difficult-and-adversarial-cases/"},{"id":"AI-27","number":27,"line":"E","title":"Blaming or changing the model without checking the whole system","short":"Debug the whole system","summary":"Diagnose model, context, retrieval, tool and orchestration failures separately, then evaluate the complete configuration.","text":"The visible symptom appears in the agent’s answer or action, but the cause may be an obsolete rule, wrong source, malformed tool response or orchestration error. A model change can hide the immediate symptom while leaving the underlying defect. Capture enough traceable evidence to reproduce the failure and test the component hypothesis before choosing a remedy. Did the system receive the right task, authorised evidence and current business rules? Were tool calls, responses and state transitions correct, and did the model use them appropriately? Can the failing case be reproduced against the exact prior configuration? Record the model, instructions, context and tool versions together with permitted task traces and outcomes. Classify failures by mechanism, form a testable diagnosis and change one relevant element at a time where feasible. Rerun the failed cases and broader acceptance suite after any important change, including apparently minor connector or source updates. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. The supplied analysis explains that model choice and agent-environment choice are different decisions. Provider-specific behaviour and integration assumptions can remain even behind a common interface. The organisation should test the complete replacement configuration, not infer portability from the connection code alone. The supplied YC example describes a data-query agent and the use of failed attempts to improve its future operation. The key pattern is the return path from a real task to a checked correction—not the assumption that recording usage automatically teaches the system what was right. Newport’s late-2025 assessment separates text-native coding environments from heterogeneous interfaces and underspecified real-world requests. The useful diagnostic questions concern hidden preferences, observable state and failures across dependent steps—not whether an agent can explain a plausible plan.","url":"/stations/27-debug-the-whole-system/"},{"id":"AI-28","number":28,"line":"E","title":"Generating work faster than people can check and use it","short":"Plan review capacity","summary":"Plan the capacity to review, accept and use output—not just the capacity to generate it.","text":"Faster generation can move the constraint into testing, expert review, publication or customer adoption. Teams may then accumulate unfinished work or approve too quickly to keep up. Measure the queue from proposal to accepted use and decide which checking can be automated, which needs expertise and how demand should be limited while that capacity is developed. Where does generated work wait, and how much is discarded or rewritten before use? Do reviewers have time and evidence to detect the failures they are responsible for catching? Has automation removed the practice through which future reviewers would learn? Map the acceptance path and review capacity before increasing generation volume; include downstream use and release gates. Automate suitable objective checks, reduce unnecessary output and prioritise work by value rather than pushing every artifact into a human queue. Staff and protect expert review, maintain supervised learning opportunities and throttle generation when meaningful inspection cannot keep pace. The essay describes how more accessible execution changes the work of deciding what matters, judging the result and carrying responsibility. This is useful for capacity planning: additional drafts or code are not automatically additional accepted output when review, prioritisation or distribution remains constrained. The compiler’s critical discussion warns that agents can produce more proposed changes than experts can inspect and that codification can remove learning opportunities for future experts. A sustainable operating model must fund both genuine review and the development of people capable of performing it. The interview distinguishes asking worthwhile questions, selecting useful outputs and committing to action. Its execution-trap discussion warns that people can become busier producing artifacts after the bottleneck has moved elsewhere. These distinctions help specify what employees must learn and what accountable reviewers must actually do. Brooks’s supplied opinion piece raises the possibility that people use saved time to take on more work and supervise more parallel activity. It is a prompt to investigate workload, concentration and learning during rollout rather than assume faster generation automatically improves employee experience.","url":"/stations/28-review-capacity-and-throughput/"},{"id":"AI-29","number":29,"line":"E","title":"Calling a demo production-ready—or estimating “90% done” by counting features","short":"Go beyond the demo","summary":"Estimate remaining uncertainty and operational work rather than the fraction of visible features built.","text":"A polished demonstration can avoid the workflows most likely to fail in production. Feature counts conceal unresolved integrations, rare exceptions, review capacity, deployment and support. Treat readiness as evidence about real operating conditions, not a percentage inferred from the easiest completed screens or tasks. Make the remaining unknowns visible before users depend on the service. Which difficult workflows, integration states and user behaviours remain untested? What support, monitoring, recovery and access work is absent from the feature list? Does the stated completion status follow from verified evidence or from a manual assertion? Identify high-uncertainty and high-consequence workflows early and test them before polishing the easy path. Use a readiness checklist covering acceptance, identity, monitoring, support, rollback and live-system constraints; name the release decision owner. Estimate remaining work from unresolved risks and dependencies, and show what is unproven rather than publish a misleading feature percentage. The addendum expands citizen development into a release decision supported by quality checks, difficult cases, security review and recovery provisions. It also separates the person who created a prototype from the person accountable for accepting it into production. The supplied 10X cheatsheet describes conventions, project specifications, active work and logs as maintained Markdown artifacts. It also distinguishes a manually asserted completion status from one derived from outstanding work. This is useful for detecting stale context and falsely complete projects. Newport’s late-2025 assessment separates text-native coding environments from heterogeneous interfaces and underspecified real-world requests. The useful diagnostic questions concern hidden preferences, observable state and failures across dependent steps—not whether an agent can explain a plausible plan.","url":"/stations/29-production-readiness-not-feature-count/"},{"id":"AI-30","number":30,"line":"E","title":"Running pilots without decisions, or committing to plans that cannot change","short":"Decide on pilots","summary":"Give each experiment a decision and keep the programme able to act on what it learns.","text":"An endless pilot consumes attention without committing to adoption or closure. A rigid programme can create the opposite failure: evidence arrives but cannot change the roadmap. Maintain a durable business direction while allowing experiments to alter scope, architecture and priorities. Define what would count as useful learning and who has authority to act on it. What decision will this pilot enable, and when will that decision be made? Which evidence would cause the team to stop, narrow the scope or choose a different approach? What must be funded and owned for a successful pilot to become sustained use? State the hypothesis, uncertainty, resource boundary, acceptance evidence and scale/adapt/stop decision before the pilot starts. Review results with business users and operating owners, including quality, adoption burden and integration effort—not only technical feasibility. Keep a visible record of changed assumptions and retired work; require a new decision when conditions or system behaviour materially change. The Commoncog note describes effectual reasoning: begin with available means, take an affordable action, learn from it and adjust as partners or constraints become clearer. This supports a pilot designed to answer a decision-relevant question rather than one expected to validate an already fixed plan. Ismail proposes an isolated AI-enabled counterpart of a predictable workflow, compared with the existing process before broader migration. The useful element is the protected comparison and staged decision; it does not require accepting the wider forecast about the future shape of companies. Pearson’s curiosity essay presents interestingness as an early search signal when the eventual application is not yet known. As a counterweight to rigid portfolio scoring, it supports reserving some bounded exploration space rather than requiring every learning activity to promise immediate financial return. The essay contrasts well-bounded, replayable environments with open organisational settings in which objectives conflict and state is incomplete. A locally successful output can still create work or harm elsewhere. Evaluation should therefore seek feedback from the people receiving the result, not only from the producing agent.","url":"/stations/30-pilots-with-decisions/"},{"id":"AI-31","number":31,"line":"F","title":"Buying external help without clear deliverables, ownership or an exit plan","short":"Plan the handover","summary":"Buy a defined outcome and the ability to operate it, not indefinite dependence on the original supplier.","text":"Discovery and implementation can both be worthwhile, but they need different deliverables. A consultant may help identify uncertainty without promising a production build; a production supplier must leave an operable system. Establish what the company will receive, control and be able to maintain before the relationship becomes essential. A handover document without access or tested rebuildability is not enough. What tangible evidence or decision must discovery deliver before more work is authorised? Which code, configuration, context, tests, data and operating records will the company control? Could another competent team diagnose and operate the service without the supplier’s private accounts or undocumented knowledge? Separate discovery, pilot and production commitments with explicit deliverables, acceptance criteria and decision points. Agree responsibilities for documentation, access, support, updates, incident response and the relevant usage/ownership rights with appropriate procurement and legal support. Test handover: have a different operator reproduce a deployment, run evaluations and perform a recovery exercise using the supplied materials. The expanded note identifies policies, company context, evaluations, tool semantics and operational records as assets to own or contractually control. The point is practical continuity: another competent team needs enough information and access to run, diagnose and change the system. Foss applies the make-or-buy question to AI itself: company-specific knowledge and integrations can create dependence on a provider even as technical access becomes easier. Procurement should examine adaptation, control and switching costs rather than compare the initial service fee alone.","url":"/stations/31-supplier-deliverables-and-handover/"},{"id":"AI-32","number":32,"line":"F","title":"Choosing a provider without understanding the cost of changing it","short":"Keep switching feasible","summary":"Understand where dependence lives and test a realistic exit path without overengineering portability.","text":"A standard API can make a technical connection easy to replace while leaving prompts, tools, evaluations, data formats and commercial rights tied to one provider. Conversely, an elaborate provider-neutral platform may cost more than the flexibility it creates. Identify the dependencies that matter for continuity, bargaining power and future improvement, then test the replacement effort that is actually relevant. Which assets and behaviours depend on the current provider rather than on the business task itself? Can the company export and use the context, configurations, evaluations and records needed to reproduce the service? What evidence would justify switching rather than remaining with an adequate existing solution? Maintain a dependency map covering technical interfaces, behavioural assumptions, data access, contracts and operational expertise. Keep business definitions, evaluations and critical context under suitable control, and test an alternative configuration on representative tasks when the exposure warrants it. Make switching decisions against expected future value, migration cost and risk—not sunk spending or model-release excitement. The supplied analysis explains that model choice and agent-environment choice are different decisions. Provider-specific behaviour and integration assumptions can remain even behind a common interface. The organisation should test the complete replacement configuration, not infer portability from the connection code alone. Foss applies the make-or-buy question to AI itself: company-specific knowledge and integrations can create dependence on a provider even as technical access becomes easier. Procurement should examine adaptation, control and switching costs rather than compare the initial service fee alone. Levie connects fragmented enterprise data with the need for company-specific context and discusses interest in multiple models. The design question is which context, tools and definitions should remain under company control when the model changes. Huber’s doctrine distinguishes company context and tools from rented model capability and commodity infrastructure. This is a useful procurement question: which assets would be required to reproduce the service or improve it with a different supplier?","url":"/stations/32-practical-provider-portability/"},{"id":"AI-33","number":33,"line":"F","title":"Waiting for users to tell you something has broken","short":"Monitor the work","summary":"Monitor whether the service is doing the job correctly, not merely whether it is online.","text":"A live endpoint can return wrong, stale or unusable results. Useful observability connects task outcomes to data freshness, tool failures, costs and human review. Alerts need accountable responders and actions; otherwise telemetry is only a growing archive. Collect enough evidence to diagnose failures without turning logs into an uncontrolled copy of sensitive company information. Which failures could remain invisible while requests continue to succeed technically? What would detect stale data, silent quality loss, rising correction time or unusual spending? Who receives each alert, how will they investigate it and what are they allowed to stop? Instrument task-level outcomes and key dependencies, including freshness, tool effects, cost and review queues; distinguish technical completion from acceptance. Define service expectations and alerts tied to actionable failure conditions, with named responders and an escalation route. Use sampled expert checks and relevant regression tests where quality is not directly observable; protect and limit sensitive traces. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. The supplied YC example describes a data-query agent and the use of failed attempts to improve its future operation. The key pattern is the return path from a real task to a checked correction—not the assumption that recording usage automatically teaches the system what was right. Rao’s conceptual distinction concerns whether acting generates usable information or instead outruns feedback and creates external costs. Applied to an agent, the question is how much it can change before the organisation can observe, interpret and contain an error.","url":"/stations/33-monitor-business-behaviour/"},{"id":"AI-34","number":34,"line":"F","title":"Launching without a failure and recovery plan","short":"Prepare recovery","summary":"Prepare to stop, contain, reconcile and continue the business when an AI-enabled workflow fails.","text":"Turning off an agent prevents some new actions but does not reveal what already happened or reverse external effects. A recovery plan must address credentials, partial work, affected records, users and business continuity. Define the fallback and test it before the old process or operational expertise disappears. For irreversible actions, recovery may mean remediation rather than rollback. Can authorised staff pause new work and revoke access without the original builder or supplier? How will the team identify completed, incomplete, duplicated and uncertain actions? What workable process serves the customer while the system is unavailable or untrusted? Create a runbook for pausing, inspecting, revoking, containing and communicating, with named decision-makers and preserved evidence. Define reconciliation and remediation for external effects, plus a manual or alternative-system fallback with sufficient capacity. Run a controlled recovery drill, including a supplier outage or staff absence, and verify that the restored service passes relevant tests before resuming. The supplied failure dossier includes unsafe retries, partial transactions, duplicate actions, poisoned context, weak identity boundaries and missing recovery. These are useful test categories because a superficially successful response can coexist with a damaged record or an action executed twice. The proposed architecture includes evaluations, searchable logs, constrained actions, review queues and rollback around sensing and execution. It is useful as a completeness check: a workflow is not governed merely because a person approved its original launch.","url":"/stations/34-failure-and-recovery/"},{"id":"AI-35","number":35,"line":"F","title":"Treating launch as the end of ownership","short":"Own the lifecycle","summary":"Fund and assign the full service lifecycle: operation, change, support, knowledge maintenance and retirement.","text":"A project may have a delivery team but no enduring owner after launch. Meanwhile models, data, tools, users and business rules change. Treat the deployed workflow as an operating service with accountable stewardship. Ownership should survive personnel changes and include the authority to restrict or retire a system whose value or safety no longer justifies its cost. Who owns the service after the project closes, and what resources are committed to that work? Who approves changes to the model, context, rules, access and evaluations? How will dependencies, credentials, retained data and user expectations be handled when the service is retired? Register every live workflow with operational and business owners, deputies, dependencies, permissions, costs and support routes. Fund recurring monitoring, expert review, regression testing, knowledge updates and user feedback; define change and incident responsibilities. Set reassessment and retirement triggers, then decommission deliberately: revoke access, stop schedules, preserve required evidence and communicate the replacement or closure. Shipper describes moving from personal agents toward agents responsible for team or company jobs, with continuing engineering and maintenance. This offers a concrete explanation for why an individual’s useful experiment still needs a service owner, shared context and ongoing support before others rely on it. The supplied analysis connects work traces to expert review, revised rules or tools, reuse and subsequent outcomes. It also treats a skill file as an operating asset with trigger conditions, permissions, expected output, tests and an escalation path—not merely a reusable prompt. The twelve-factor note includes evaluation rubrics, retained operational traces and encoded expertise as parts of a learning system. Together they suggest that trace collection is useful only when the organisation can interpret outcomes and revise a tested process.","url":"/stations/35-lifecycle-ownership/"},{"id":"AI-36","number":36,"line":"F","title":"Assuming one success transfers unchanged across the company or portfolio","short":"Revalidate at scale","summary":"Reuse proven assets while revalidating local workflows, data, economics, authority and adoption.","text":"A successful implementation provides a useful starting point, not a universal template. Another company may have different definitions, exceptions, system access, review capacity or customer expectations. Separate the reusable technical and learning assets from assumptions that were local to the first setting. Scale through evidence about the new context rather than through executive enthusiasm alone. Which parts of the original success depended on local data, people, incentives or system architecture? What is genuinely reusable, and what must be re-specified or tested in the new setting? Does the second organisation have the ownership and support capacity required to sustain the approach? Package reusable components with their scope, assumptions, tests, known failures and operating requirements—not only code or a success story. Run a local fit assessment and bounded validation using the receiving team’s cases, definitions, permissions and baseline. Share failures and adaptations across the portfolio, then revise the common pattern without forcing every business into identical work practices. The supplied scenarios contrast companies using similar systems but having different process clarity and decision discipline. This gives a practical reason to check local workflow maturity before copying another implementation, even when both businesses use the same customer-management platform. The structured note describes distributed intelligence as depending on shared cross-functional context and a culture reinforced through rewards, roles and actual decisions. Granting nominal autonomy without those supports may simply relocate coordination work to overloaded local teams. Foss argues that firms and markets must be compared as ways to govern specific transactions. Adaptation when conditions change, relationship-specific investments and difficult-to-measure performance remain relevant even when exchanging information becomes easier. This challenges any automatic inference from AI capability to a flat organisation or universal outsourcing. The supplied Cybernetic Teammate research note reports a randomised product-innovation exercise in which AI-assisted individuals could perform comparably to unassisted teams on assessed ideas. This is a useful example of testing work quality rather than counting usage, while preserving the boundary between an exercise and sustained organisational performance.","url":"/stations/36-revalidate-when-scaling/"}],"previews":[{"id":"AI-01","number":1,"line":"A","title":"Strategy, not licences","summary":"Define the business change before treating tool access as progress.","url":"/stations/01-licences-are-not-strategy/"},{"id":"AI-02","number":2,"line":"A","title":"Understand the work","summary":"Map real work, including its exceptions and informal decisions, before deciding what to automate.","url":"/stations/02-understand-before-automating/"},{"id":"AI-03","number":3,"line":"A","title":"Value over spectacle","summary":"Choose useful problems and make room for bounded discovery—not just impressive demonstrations.","url":"/stations/03-value-before-spectacle/"},{"id":"AI-04","number":4,"line":"A","title":"Use simpler software","summary":"Use AI where interpretation helps and ordinary software where explicit rules are sufficient.","url":"/stations/04-simplest-effective-system/"},{"id":"AI-05","number":5,"line":"A","title":"Measure outcomes","summary":"Connect usage to accepted work and accepted work to an actual business outcome.","url":"/stations/05-measure-business-improvement/"},{"id":"AI-06","number":6,"line":"A","title":"Prove the economics","summary":"Establish capability inside a budget, then optimise the complete cost of successful delivery.","url":"/stations/06-economics-after-feasibility/"},{"id":"AI-07","number":7,"line":"B","title":"Business ownership","summary":"Join executive sponsorship, process ownership and technical delivery without pretending one role can do all three.","url":"/stations/07-business-ownership/"},{"id":"AI-08","number":8,"line":"B","title":"Avoid the bottleneck","summary":"Centralise reusable support and essential controls while keeping local outcomes and workflow knowledge close to the work.","url":"/stations/08-central-team-without-bottleneck/"},{"id":"AI-09","number":9,"line":"B","title":"Learn in real work","summary":"Develop role-specific capability through real tasks, feedback and protected practice.","url":"/stations/09-learning-in-real-work/"},{"id":"AI-10","number":10,"line":"B","title":"Govern the prototype","summary":"Give non-technical builders a supported route from useful experiment to owned, tested service.","url":"/stations/10-prototype-to-production/"},{"id":"AI-11","number":11,"line":"B","title":"Bring risk partners in","summary":"Design a workable route for responsible experimentation with the people who own its constraints.","url":"/stations/11-risk-partners-from-start/"},{"id":"AI-12","number":12,"line":"B","title":"Rebuild trust","summary":"Address trust, incentives, workload and role uncertainty rather than assume another course will solve adoption.","url":"/stations/12-resistance-incentives-trust/"},{"id":"AI-13","number":13,"line":"B","title":"Keep your judgement","summary":"Keep the capacity to judge the work, challenge its assumptions and accept responsibility for its consequences.","url":"/stations/13-retain-judgement/"},{"id":"AI-14","number":14,"line":"C","title":"Fix useful data","summary":"Prepare the data needed for the chosen use case without making every project depend on a complete enterprise rebuild.","url":"/stations/14-use-case-data-foundation/"},{"id":"AI-15","number":15,"line":"C","title":"Define the terms","summary":"Make business meaning, calculation rules and source authority explicit for each question.","url":"/stations/15-shared-business-definitions/"},{"id":"AI-16","number":16,"line":"C","title":"Know the blind spots","summary":"Make the boundary of the evidence visible: coverage, missingness, history and freshness.","url":"/stations/16-data-coverage-and-limits/"},{"id":"AI-17","number":17,"line":"C","title":"Capture the know-how","summary":"Capture decision-relevant expertise while preserving the people and practice that keep it current.","url":"/stations/17-capture-and-renew-expertise/"},{"id":"AI-18","number":18,"line":"C","title":"Retrieve what matters","summary":"Provide permission-aware retrieval that returns the right evidence and makes retrieval failures diagnosable.","url":"/stations/18-relevant-retrieval/"},{"id":"AI-19","number":19,"line":"C","title":"Test integrations","summary":"Test business semantics and partial effects, not just successful API connections.","url":"/stations/19-integration-behaviour/"},{"id":"AI-20","number":20,"line":"D","title":"Enforce permissions","summary":"Enforce what an agent may read and do in software, using identities and permissions appropriate to its job.","url":"/stations/20-enforced-agent-permissions/"},{"id":"AI-21","number":21,"line":"D","title":"Protect data by design","summary":"Make approved data handling understandable and supported by technical controls across the information lifecycle.","url":"/stations/21-data-protection-by-design/"},{"id":"AI-22","number":22,"line":"D","title":"Distrust outside instructions","summary":"Treat retrieved documents, messages and tool results as evidence to inspect—not as authority to change the agent’s rules.","url":"/stations/22-untrusted-instructions/"},{"id":"AI-23","number":23,"line":"D","title":"Set hard limits","summary":"Bound the amount of work, money and external effect an agent can authorise before the system stops or seeks approval.","url":"/stations/23-bounded-agent-execution/"},{"id":"AI-24","number":24,"line":"D","title":"Match controls to risk","summary":"Calibrate autonomy and assurance to each decision’s consequences, uncertainty and reversibility.","url":"/stations/24-controls-proportionate-to-risk/"},{"id":"AI-25","number":25,"line":"E","title":"Define good results","summary":"Turn business expectations into representative cases, acceptance criteria and boundaries for abstention.","url":"/stations/25-define-good-results/"},{"id":"AI-26","number":26,"line":"E","title":"Test the hard cases","summary":"Use domain users, exceptions and adversarial cases to test the boundaries of the entire workflow.","url":"/stations/26-test-difficult-and-adversarial-cases/"},{"id":"AI-27","number":27,"line":"E","title":"Debug the whole system","summary":"Diagnose model, context, retrieval, tool and orchestration failures separately, then evaluate the complete configuration.","url":"/stations/27-debug-the-whole-system/"},{"id":"AI-28","number":28,"line":"E","title":"Plan review capacity","summary":"Plan the capacity to review, accept and use output—not just the capacity to generate it.","url":"/stations/28-review-capacity-and-throughput/"},{"id":"AI-29","number":29,"line":"E","title":"Go beyond the demo","summary":"Estimate remaining uncertainty and operational work rather than the fraction of visible features built.","url":"/stations/29-production-readiness-not-feature-count/"},{"id":"AI-30","number":30,"line":"E","title":"Decide on pilots","summary":"Give each experiment a decision and keep the programme able to act on what it learns.","url":"/stations/30-pilots-with-decisions/"},{"id":"AI-31","number":31,"line":"F","title":"Plan the handover","summary":"Buy a defined outcome and the ability to operate it, not indefinite dependence on the original supplier.","url":"/stations/31-supplier-deliverables-and-handover/"},{"id":"AI-32","number":32,"line":"F","title":"Keep switching feasible","summary":"Understand where dependence lives and test a realistic exit path without overengineering portability.","url":"/stations/32-practical-provider-portability/"},{"id":"AI-33","number":33,"line":"F","title":"Monitor the work","summary":"Monitor whether the service is doing the job correctly, not merely whether it is online.","url":"/stations/33-monitor-business-behaviour/"},{"id":"AI-34","number":34,"line":"F","title":"Prepare recovery","summary":"Prepare to stop, contain, reconcile and continue the business when an AI-enabled workflow fails.","url":"/stations/34-failure-and-recovery/"},{"id":"AI-35","number":35,"line":"F","title":"Own the lifecycle","summary":"Fund and assign the full service lifecycle: operation, change, support, knowledge maintenance and retirement.","url":"/stations/35-lifecycle-ownership/"},{"id":"AI-36","number":36,"line":"F","title":"Revalidate at scale","summary":"Reuse proven assets while revalidating local workflows, data, economics, authority and adoption.","url":"/stations/36-revalidate-when-scaling/"}]};